# YOUR NEOVIM: TEN SHORT LESSONS *jc-start*

Learn by doing, then recall the keys without looking. About 30 minutes;
split it into several five-minute sessions if you prefer.

START: run :Keycourse in a new jc-nvim session. It opens this guide on
the left and a temporary practice.lua on the right, in a separate tab.
Only edit that temporary project. :pwd shows its location. No real project
is copied, committed, or pushed. Existing tabs keep their working directory.

This guide is an editable, non-writable Tutor buffer: your answers do not
change the installed lesson. :Keycourse returns to its tab; :Tutor
jc-nvim-reference opens the [complete key reference](@tutor:jc-nvim-reference).
Start a new Neovim session for a completely fresh workbook.

NOTATION: <Space> is your leader. <C-x> means hold Ctrl and press x;
<M-x> means Alt+x. grn means three consecutive keys, not a chord.
Uppercase matters. N = Normal, I = Insert, V = Visual, O = pending operator.
Type leader sequences promptly: your mapping timeout is 300 ms.

PRACTICE: use Ctrl+Right to enter the code window and Ctrl+Left to return.
The tasks are self-checked. Each CHECK line is an automatic recall quiz:
place the cursor on its final ?, type $cl, type the literal answer, then Esc.
Its gutter sign changes when correct. Read a lesson's answer only if stuck.
In this Tutor buffer Enter follows links and >> executes the current line;
do editing/indentation drills in practice.lua, NOT on instruction lines.

Choose a lesson with Enter on its link:
- [1. Move and discover](*jc-move*)
- [2. Edit without losing your yank](*jc-edit*)
- [3. Find files and retrace your steps](*jc-files*)
- [4. Search and replace](*jc-search*)
- [5. Splits and bookmarks](*jc-windows*)
- [6. Select code and jump with labels](*jc-code*)
- [7. Understand and refactor code](*jc-lsp*)
- [8. Complete, wrap, and use AI](*jc-completion*)
- [9. Manage files with netrw](*jc-netrw*)
- [10. Git, maintenance, and a final challenge](*jc-git*)

## 1. MOVE AND DISCOVER *jc-move*

In the code window, press Esc. Use h/j/k/l or arrows, w/b for words,
0/$ for line ends, gg/G for file ends, and 5j to move five lines.
i enters Insert; Esc or Ctrl+c returns to Normal in your config.
v selects characters, V selects lines, Ctrl+v selects a block.
u undoes; Ctrl+r redoes; . repeats a repeatable edit. :w saves.

TRY: open the keymap picker with <Space>sk, search for "history", then
Ctrl+c to close. Open <Space>sh, search for "text-objects", and Enter.
Close just that help window with :q. These are your memory aids.

Normal PageDown/PageUp scroll half a page and center, not a full page.
Your screen shows relative line numbers to help with counted motions.

RECALL: which personalized shortcut lists keybindings? [Answer](*jc-answers*)
CHECK 1: ?

## 2. EDIT WITHOUT LOSING YOUR YANK *jc-edit*

On the WORDS comment, put the cursor on red and type yiw (yank a word).
Move to green, type viw, then p. EXPECT: red red blue. Undo once.
Now use ciw to change green to gold. Your previous yank is still red.

Your d, D, c, C discard old text into the black-hole register (_); deletion is
not cutting. Yank explicitly before moving text. D/C in Visual mode can
affect whole lines, not just highlighted characters. u can undo mistakes.

TRY: V-select the three MOVE comments, Shift+Up then Shift+Down.
Press > then <; the selection stays active. Esc to finish.
Normal > or < shifts the current line immediately; >> would shift twice
in a code buffer. J joins two lines while preserving cursor position;
try it on the JOIN comment, then undo. Counts on this custom J are lost.

CAUTION: Visual Shift+Left/Right are also configured, but can delete the
selection at line boundaries. Do not rely on them; undo immediately if used.

RECALL: which register do your d/c mappings use? [Answer](*jc-answers*)
CHECK 2: ?

## 3. FIND FILES AND RETRACE YOUR STEPS *jc-files*

TRY: <Space>sf, type "other", Enter. Then <Space><Space> and choose
practice.lua. Home returns to other.lua; End returns to practice.lua.
These keys follow FILE history, not line boundaries or cursor jumps.
They support counts and skip special/deleted buffers. Do not go farther
back into your real files during practice.

In Telescope: Ctrl+n/p or arrows select; Enter opens; Ctrl+c closes.
Esc leaves picker Insert mode, then another Esc closes it.
Ctrl+x/v/t opens in a horizontal split/vertical split/tab.
Tab/Shift+Tab toggle multi-selection; Ctrl+u/d scroll the preview.
Ctrl+/ in Insert, or ? in picker Normal mode, shows its keys.
Ctrl+j is disabled; Ctrl+k scrolls the preview sideways, not up a result.

Other launchers: <Space>s. recent files; <Space>sp tracked Git files;
<Space>sc your config; <Space>sr resume; <Space>st list pickers;
<Space>sn notifications. Inspect real config read-only if you open it.
Git files may be empty until you stage a sandbox file in lesson 10.

RECALL: which key goes to the previous FILE? [Answer](*jc-answers*)
CHECK 3: ?

## 4. SEARCH AND REPLACE *jc-search*

TRY in practice.lua: /banana then Enter. n goes to the next match,
N goes back; both center and open folds. Lowercase searches ignore case;
adding uppercase makes a search case-sensitive. Esc clears highlighting.

Use <Space>/ for fuzzy search in this buffer, <Space>sw for the current
word across files, <Space>sg for project live grep, and <Space>s/ for
live grep in open files. Search "greet". Close the picker with Ctrl+c.

Put the cursor on banana and press <Space>r. The command line is ready
for the REPLACEMENT: type mango and Enter. EXPECT: all three whole-word
banana occurrences in this file become mango. Undo after inspecting.
This is case-sensitive textual replacement, not a language-aware rename.
Esc cancels the prompt. An empty replacement deletes the matching words.

Telescope Ctrl+q sends all results to QUICKFIX; Alt+q sends selections.
Use :cnext/:cprevious there. Your <Space>n/p keys use a LOCATION LIST
instead; they do not navigate quickfix. Lesson 7 shows their intended use.

RECALL: which shortcut prefills whole-word replacement? [Answer](*jc-answers*)
CHECK 4: ?

## 5. SPLITS AND BOOKMARKS *jc-windows*

TRY in the code window: :vsplit other.lua, then Ctrl+Left/Ctrl+Right.
Use :q to close ONLY the extra split. :split opens a horizontal split.
Ctrl+Up/Down moves vertically; Ctrl+Backslash returns to the previous
window/pane. At an editor edge these can enter an adjacent tmux pane.
These mappings also work in Insert, Visual, and terminal-input modes.

CAUTION: Ctrl+Backslash also shadows the usual terminal-mode escape
Ctrl+Backslash Ctrl+n. Do not open a Neovim terminal for this course.
The reference explains this existing limitation; this lesson changes no keys.

OPTIONAL HARPOON: with the temporary project active, use <Space>ma on
practice.lua and other.lua. <Space>mf opens the saved-file menu;
Enter opens its row, q/Esc closes. Alt+1 through Alt+9 selects list slots.
The list persists. Remove these temporary entries in the menu afterward
with dd, then q. To reorder: yy BEFORE dd, then P, because d discards text.

RECALL: which chord moves into the right split? [Answer](*jc-answers*)
CHECK 5: ?

## 6. SELECT CODE AND JUMP WITH LABELS *jc-code*

In practice.lua, move inside greet. Type vif, inspect the selected function
body, then Esc. Compare vaf (whole function, linewise). Try via/vaa on
a parameter. as selects local scope where the parser's query supports it.
Combine objects with y/d/c: e.g. yif or cia. Only mutate the sandbox.

TRY: ]f and [f move to next/previous function starts. Open model.py and
try ]c/[c plus vic/vac. Your outer-class selection is BLOCKWISE.
Parser/query support varies; skip unavailable objects instead of guessing.
These structure movements do not add entries to the jumplist.

TRY: zk, type a few letters, then a displayed label to jump. Esc cancels.
zt selects syntax ranges. In Normal/Visual, Ctrl+Space starts incremental
range selection; press it again to expand, Backspace to shrink, Esc to exit.
f/F/t/T and ;/, are also Flash-enhanced character jumps/repeats.
Advanced: yzr, choose a label, then iw yanks a remote word and returns;
yzs searches a syntax range. zr/zs apply in Visual/operator-pending modes.
With a parser, try za to toggle a fold; zo/zc open/close; zR/zM all.

RECALL: select the inside of a function in Visual mode. [Answer](*jc-answers*)
CHECK 6: ?

## 7. UNDERSTAND AND REFACTOR CODE *jc-lsp*

Use the Lua files. Wait for a language server; :checkhealth vim.lsp can
diagnose missing support. No server? Read this lesson and skip its drills.
TRY: on greet in other.lua, grd opens its definition; K shows hover.
grr lists references; gri implementations; grt type definitions;
grD declarations. Not every symbol/server supports every operation.
gO lists document symbols; gW searches workspace symbols.

In this DISPOSABLE project, grn can rename greet to welcome. Visit each
changed sandbox file and :w to save. Never :wall: it can save real buffers.
gra requests a code action (Normal or Visual). <Space>f formats this buffer;
saving normally formats configured languages too. <Space>th toggles buffer
inlay hints when supported. Refactors can change multiple files; stay here.

TRY: add a typo such as missing_course_name() to a Lua file. If diagnostics
appear, ]d/[d moves next/previous; ]D/[D last/first; Ctrl+w d opens details.
<Space>sd searches diagnostics. <Space>q opens/closes this buffer's
diagnostic location list; <Space>n/p moves next/previous and centers.
Remove the typo. Empty diagnostics are fine; they are not a failed lesson.

RECALL: language-aware rename, rather than text replacement? [Answer](*jc-answers*)
CHECK 7: ?

## 8. COMPLETE, WRAP, AND USE AI *jc-completion*

In practice.lua Insert mode, type part of a known name. Ctrl+Space opens
Blink completion; Ctrl+n/p or arrows choose; Ctrl+y ACCEPTS.
Ctrl+e cancels. Enter inserts a newline; Tab is NOT completion accept.
Ctrl+b/f scroll docs (Ctrl+f has an AI exception below); Ctrl+k toggles
signature help. In the : command line, Tab/Shift+Tab cycles completion.

TRY: accept a snippet such as a function snippet. Tab advances a placeholder,
Shift+Tab goes back. Otherwise Tab tries a Sidekick edit, then tabout or
ordinary Tab. Tabout can cross closing delimiters when a parser is available.
Autopairs inserts matching quotes/brackets; Backspace can remove an empty
pair, and Enter handles a pair-aware newline.

TRY in Normal: on a plain word, siw) wraps it in parentheses; undo.
siw( adds interior spaces instead. s takes a motion then a delimiter.
In Insert, Ctrl+l then ) inserts a pair at the cursor; Ctrl+l Ctrl+l
then ) puts the pair on separate lines. ds/cs and Visual S are NOT enabled.

OPTIONAL AI (needs Copilot access/network): with visible ghost text,
Ctrl+f accepts all, Ctrl+d a line, Ctrl+s a word, Ctrl+g cycles suggestions.
Blink hides ghost text while its menu is open. Normal Ctrl+i jumps/applies
Sidekick's next edit; otherwise it is native jump-forward. Esc clears NES.
There are no configured Sidekick CLI launcher shortcuts. Skip AI if offline.

RECALL: the completion-menu accept chord? [Answer](*jc-answers*)
CHECK 8: ?

## 9. MANAGE FILES WITH NETRW *jc-netrw*

From practice.lua press <Space>e: netrw opens its directory and focuses
the filename. Enter opens a file/directory; - goes to its parent;
<Space>e toggles back. Ctrl+c closes the netrw buffer.

TRY ONLY IN THE TEMP PROJECT: :edit files, then N and enter draft.txt.
N refuses to overwrite an existing file and can create parent directories.
Move to draft.txt; R renames it, D deletes it (read the prompts).
Use d for a directory. Never practice these on your actual config files.

Literal mf marks/unmarks a file; mF clears current-listing marks.
Your Tab/Shift+Tab mappings INTEND to do that but are nonrecursive:
they currently set ordinary Vim marks instead. Use mf/mF, not Tab.
<Space>r REFRESHES here; it is not word replacement. Ctrl+h/l follow
the jumplist, while u/U follows netrw directory history.
o/v/t/p opens in split/vsplit/tab/preview; i cycles list style,
I toggles banner, gh toggles dotfiles, s changes sorting, qf shows info.
Use long listing for these exercises; wider layouts have plugin limitations.

RECALL: working netrw file-mark toggle? [Answer](*jc-answers*)
CHECK 9: ?

## 10. GIT, MAINTENANCE, AND A FINAL CHALLENGE *jc-git*

The launcher initializes a temporary Git repo when Git is installed, without
commits or remotes. In a practice file press <Space>gf for Fugitive status.
Choose an untracked file, s stages it; find it under Staged, then = shows
its diff, u unstages it, gq closes. Here s is STAGE; g? shows local keys.
Git actions are real: do not use X (discard), amend, reset, or push as drills.

REFERENCE ONLY: <Space>R restarts Neovim; <Space>ps updates plugins.
Do not run either just to pass a lesson. :Mason manages external tools;
:ConformInfo explains formatting; :checkhealth reports setup issues.
Native gcc/gc{motion}/Visual gc comments code; gqq may invoke LSP format,
whereas gw reflows text without formatexpr. No custom Gitsigns hunk keys
are configured. Consult the reference for optional plugin-local controls.

FINAL CHALLENGE, without looking back: find other.lua, open its definition,
return through file history, wrap one word, find a project-wide string,
open a split, accept a completion, and create a file in the sandbox.
Skip server/AI-dependent parts if unavailable. Repeat tomorrow from memory.

RECALL: open Git status from a code buffer. [Answer](*jc-answers*)
CHECK 10: ?

FINISH: remove any temporary Harpoon marks, then :tabclose! closes the
course tab (or quit this dedicated Neovim session). The practice files are
temporary; don't keep work there. Quizzes do not persist to the source file.
You can always reopen the [full reference](@tutor:jc-nvim-reference).

